Tooling, workflows, metrics dashboard, and command-centre design
Tool choice: Google-first vs Microsoft-first vs hybrid (decision table)
This table compares stacks using criteria that matter for institution-first, teacher-centred implementation.

Criterion	Google-first (Workspace for Education)	Microsoft-first (Microsoft 365 Education)	Hybrid approach
Core collaboration surface	Core services include Gmail, Drive, Docs, Forms, Classroom, Meet, Sites, etc. 
Teams, Word/Excel/PowerPoint web, OneNote, etc; education AI materials emphasize Copilot Chat + efficiency + security posture. 
Use one as primary identity + repository; integrate the other only if needed
Privacy posture baseline	Core services: no ads; school-managed accounts; privacy notice clarifies core vs additional services. 
Education AI pages position Copilot Chat and security; training emphasizes readiness and secure management. 
Higher governance complexity; avoid unless you have strong admin capacity
Teacher training availability	Large ecosystem; training varies by region and partner	Microsoft Educator Center + AI learning paths; programs and PD hours emphasized. 
Requires dual training tracks
Offline/low bandwidth resilience	Docs/Drive offline modes depend on device setup; DIKSHA can complement for PD and content. 
Teams and web apps depend on connectivity; can still operate with local docs + periodic sync	Hybrid can worsen low-bandwidth experience
Interoperability / portability	Exportable documents; institutional ownership can be configured	Exportable documents; stronger integration if school already uses Microsoft	Risk of split-brain repository
Recommended for Lamahatta pilot	Prefer the stack teachers will actually use daily without friction	Prefer if school already uses Microsoft identities or has strong IT support	Use hybrid only if there’s a clear need

Interpretation: choose the first stack based on staff familiarity and available admin capacity; your “institution online” outcome depends more on governance and habits than on vendor choice.

Responsible AI staff policy templates grounded in DPDP obligations
Even in a teacher/institution-only phase, staff will generate documents and may handle personal data (staff phone numbers, HR records, meeting notes). DPDP Act principles relevant to your school policy include:

consent and notice requirements for processing (structure of notices and consent quality), 
“reasonable security safeguards” to prevent personal data breach, 
breach intimation obligations, 
and special protections around children (important to pre-commit now, even if student focus is later). 
A tight staff policy should therefore include:

Data classification: public / internal / confidential.
AI usage boundaries: which classes of data may never be input into AI tools.
Verification rule: “AI drafts; humans decide.”
Breach response: a one-page incident workflow and contact chain.
Access control: role-based access + 2-admin recovery.
Teacher PD pathway levels 1–4 (curriculum + session plan + rubric)
NEP specifies CPD expectations and explicitly includes online teacher development modules and platforms to share best practices. 
 A Level 1–4 pathway ensures that CPD does not remain “tool training,” but becomes measurable capability-building.

Level 1: Institutional basics (accounts + repository habits)
Goal: every participating teacher can use official identity and store/share institutional documents.

Session 1 (90 min): accounts, password hygiene, login recovery (no personal accounts for institutional work).
Session 2 (90 min): shared drive structure, naming conventions, upload/download, offline access basics.
Assessment rubric (pass/fail + proficiency):
Can create and share a document with correct permissions.
Can find and reuse an existing template.
Can submit a weekly plan via the shared repository.
Level 2: Workflow mastery (5 core workflows)
Goal: reduce workload via repeatable routines.

Session 3 (75 min): meeting minutes + action tracking workflow.
Session 4 (75 min): forms-based assessment + auto summaries.
Session 5 (75 min): circular drafting + archive + retrieval.
Rubric:

Completes workflow without help.
Uses template correctly.
Produces artifacts that are reusable.
Level 3: Responsible AI for staff (low-risk first)
Goal: safe productivity gains with verification.

Grounding: UNESCO emphasizes inclusion/equity and policy governance for AI in education. 
 OECD principles reinforce trustworthy AI aligned to rights and democratic values. 

Session 6 (90 min): “Allowed vs Restricted” AI uses; data handling; hallucination and verification.
Session 7 (60 min): prompt patterns for lesson planning, rubrics, feedback banks (teacher verifies).
Rubric:
Never inputs personal data into public AI.
Verifies outputs against textbook/official sources.
Produces a lesson artifact with a verification note.
Level 4: Champion capability (train-the-trainer + sustainment)
Goal: the school sustains practice without external push.

Session 8 (90 min): facilitator skills, micro-coaching, measuring adoption.
Session 9 (60 min): community rituals, template curation, onboarding new teachers.
Rubric:
Runs weekly ritual.
Helps another teacher complete a workflow.
Adds at least 5 reusable assets to the library.
Metrics dashboard design and sample charts
Your dashboard should be “command-centre ready,” but lightweight enough that teachers don’t resent it.

KPI set grounded in school realities (UDISE + NEP)
UDISE+ data indicates partial internet/computer facility coverage; thus, you must track institution readiness and adoption, not just “AI usage.” 
 NEP also expects technology use for planning and management. 

